The bias is the amount of assumptions that the model is making against a certain problem you are trying to frame. 
one model with a very high bias is linear regression, because it assumes that data have a linear distribution.
WHen a model has high bias it underfits.
The variance is the sensitivity of the model. How much the output would have changed if you changed the data?
A model with high variance is dependent on the subset of real world data you are training it on.
If the subset of data change, the output change a lot, and it shouldn't 
what do we mean by subset of data?
A model with high variance is the decision tree. If you don't put a limit to its growth,
it will branch up as far as possible to fit all and every single data point.
On the training set it might give good results, but in the real world it would fail to perform.

A solution to underfitting might be to train the model more if it has not been trained enough, so more training might help it 
to converge to a solution.

If the model is simple and the problem is high bias with underfitting, increasing the complexity of the model might be the good choice.
Or change architecture directly.

If the model is overfitting, introducing more data might be useful, as it would increase data diversity so that the model doesn't closely
resemble all the data points anymore.

Regularization is a good solution to overfitting, because it decrease the complexity, the flexibility of the model so that it 
less capable of following all the datapoints, and so, generalize better. 



Is it a neural network capable  of  solving a linear regression?

Yes — a neural network can absolutely solve a linear regression problem, and in fact, it can learn exactly the same solution as ordinary linear regression if it's configured correctly.

A neural network with:

No hidden layers

Linear activation (or no activation at all)

A single output neuron

...is mathematically identical to linear regression.

